  - slide : {"description": "The pill slides from the word before to the word being said, over 0.2 s or sooner, instead of jumping: writes word_style.active_pill.glide true on every page placed. Default false, and never what the sheet last chose. It applies with style pill: true is refused by name with any other style or with place false, where it would show nothing; false is always accepted (unlike replace, which is refused whenever it is given where it cannot apply).", "type": "boolean"}
  - language : {"type": "string", "description": "A language tag, e.g. en-US. WITH apple: this Mac's own language by default. The two ways a tag can be wrong are two different problems with two different fixes, so they are refused SEPARATELY and the message says which you have. A tag speech recognition has never heard of is a misspelling: it is refused with the tags it does know. A tag it knows whose on-device MODEL is not installed on this Mac is refused as not installed, with the languages ready here: a person has to install it from the Generate Captions sheet, and this tool never downloads. WITH assemblyai: \"auto\" (the default) detects it and the result reports what was detected and how sure it was; a code is passed through unchecked, and one AssemblyAI does not support comes back as its own sentence after the upload."}
  - clips : {"description": "Transcribe only these clips — the sheet's scope. Omit for every clip in the project that has recorded sound. Only a clip playing a movie or sound file can be transcribed, and not one played in REVERSE, whose words would come out backwards; a clip you name that cannot be is reported under skipped.", "type": "array", "minItems": 1, "items": {"type": "integer"}}
  - refresh : {"description": "engine \"assemblyai\" only. Ignore the cached transcript and upload again (default false). Refused by name with engine \"apple\", which has no cache.", "type": "boolean"}
  - place : {"type": "boolean", "description": "Place the cues as text clips (default true), as ONE undo step. false returns the cues and changes nothing at all — no edit, no undo step — which is the way to check a transcript before committing to it."}
  - speakers : {"description": "engine \"assemblyai\" only. Ask for speaker labels (default false); a change of speaker then starts a new cue, and with style subtitles each speaker's cues take their own colour (white, yellow, cyan, green in order of first appearance, then again; a speaker is a label within one media file). Refused by name with engine \"apple\".", "type": "boolean"}
  - dim : {"description": "The page fades to half its opacity in a pause of 0.25 s or more: writes word_style.pause_dim 0.5 on every page placed. On-device (engine apple) words rarely carry a pause that long, so it shows mostly with assemblyai. Default false, and never what the sheet last chose. It applies with style highlight, pill, karaoke, build or one_word: true is refused by name with any other style or with place false, where it would show nothing; false is always accepted (unlike replace, which is refused whenever it is given where it cannot apply).", "type": "boolean"}
  - replace : {"description": "With a word style and place true only — refused by name with subtitles or with place false. true removes, in the SAME undo step, every caption track this command could have made over these clips: named Captions or Captions N, holding only text clips, each of which overlaps a clip being transcribed. The result lists them under replaced.", "type": "boolean"}
  - engine : {"type": "string", "description": "Where the recognition happens: apple (default) on this Mac, assemblyai in the cloud. Anything else is refused with both spellings named. The default is NEVER what the sheet last chose — an agent that wants the cloud says so every time.", "enum": ["apple", "assemblyai"]}
  - fill : {"description": "The word being said fills left to right as it is spoken, taking 0.6 s at most: writes word_style.active_fill true on every page placed. Default false, and never what the sheet last chose. It applies with style highlight, karaoke or build: true is refused by name with any other style or with place false, where it would show nothing; false is always accepted (unlike replace, which is refused whenever it is given where it cannot apply).", "type": "boolean"}
## export : Starts writing the timeline to a file and answers AT ONCE with {export_id, path, status} — ⌘E, the export sheet's six controls (format, size, frame rate, quality, loudness, range) in the sheet's own vocabulary, all but the frame rate — this exports at the document's rate, or on gif and apng at the format's own rate (12.5 and 25 fps). The export is a job in the Export Queue, run one at a time behind any other export already there; status is queued or running. An av1, av1_webm or vp9_webm job also waits while the app's other AV1 and VP9 exports would hold more than a quarter of this Mac's memory with it, and a later job may run first meanwhile (export_status's waiting says so). Follow it with export_status, which never waits — call it again after a pause until status is done, failed or cancelled — and stop it with cancel_export. The export_id lasts until Frame Job quits. Every refusal comes back from this call, before anything is queued: a bad argument, a missing folder, and a graphic clip whose module does not compile; a failure while writing comes back from export_status, failed with its reason (a graphic that fails during the export stops it with no file left, naming the graphic and the frame). The preset is the ENCODING only and the size a separate choice defaulting to the document's own, or on gif and apng to the format's cap; the core resolves both into concrete settings (container, codec, size, rate, range) and hands them to the same exporter the sheet uses, so an agent export and a menu export cannot disagree. An existing file at the path is replaced, and the container's extension is added when the path has none. In an HLG project (set_canvas output) hevc_10bit, av1, av1_webm, vp9_webm, prores_422 and prores_4444 write HLG unless dynamic_range says pq or sdr, and every other video preset writes the project's SDR version. What the finished file holds is export_status's done answer.
  - peak_dbtp : {"type": "number", "minimum": -20, "maximum": 0, "description": "The ceiling of the master limiter every export with sound runs, in dBTP: the file's true peak stays at or under it. Omit for -1 (the sheet's fixed value); 0 is the loosest, and there is no off. Legal with or without loudness_lufs. How far the limiter turned the hottest peak down is the result's limited_db."}
  - audio_bits : {"description": "audio_wav's and audio_aiff's bit depth — the sheet's Bit depth popup: 16 (the default) or 24, rounded once from the float mix, never dithered. Refused on every other preset (ProRes keeps 16).", "type": "integer", "enum": [16, 24]}
  - preset : {"type": "string", "description": "Export preset id — one per encoding, a container and a codec with no size of its own (that is size's). h264 is what the export sheet opens on. Four rows keep a transparent canvas transparent: prores_4444, hevc_alpha and vp9_alpha_webm (the movie rows — see size, which constrains them) and apng. gif is not one of them: it can show a transparent pixel but has no alpha channel, so nothing partial survives it. prores_422, prores_4444 and hevc_10bit keep the compositor's 16-bit frames at 10 bits (ProRes 4444 at 12); h264, hevc and hevc_alpha are 8-bit. av1 is AV1 in an MP4 with AAC and av1_webm the same AV1 in a WebM with Opus, both 10-bit, encoded by the bundled SVT-AV1 at encoder_speed; Macs before M3 cannot play them back. vp9_webm is VP9 in a WebM with Opus from the bundled libvpx, at one speed: 8-bit (Profile 0) for an SDR picture, 10-bit (Profile 2) for HLG and PQ. vp9_alpha_webm is that VP9 with its alpha, always 8-bit (the SDR version in an HLG project), the transparent movie Chrome and Firefox play; Safari's is hevc_alpha. Pass a wrong one and the error lists them all."}
  - dynamic_range : {"description": "An HDR (HLG) project's picture on hevc_10bit, av1, av1_webm, vp9_webm, prores_422 and prores_4444 — the sheet's Dynamic Range popup: hlg (the default; BT.2100 HLG, no Dolby Vision), pq (HDR10: BT.2100 PQ at 1,000 cd/m², mastering display P3-D65 0.005–1,000 cd/m², MaxCLL 1,000, MaxFALL 0) or sdr (the SDR version). Every other video preset (vp9_alpha_webm too) writes the SDR version; hlg and pq are refused there and in an SDR project, where sdr changes nothing.", "type": "string", "enum": ["hlg", "pq", "sdr"]}
  - loudness_lufs : {"maximum": 0, "type": "number", "minimum": -70, "description": "Normalise the whole mix to this integrated loudness, in LUFS — the sheet's Loudness popup, whose rows are -14 (streaming), -16 (podcast) and -23 (EBU R 128 broadcast). The export measures itself first and then applies ONE gain to everything, solved through the master limiter every export runs at peak_dbtp, so the file lands on the target and its loudest peaks are limited rather than the whole mix capped. Omit not to normalise: the export still measures, still limits at the ceiling and still reports. Ignored where there is no sound to measure (gif, apng)."}
  - size : {"maxItems": 2, "minItems": 2, "description": "Output size — the sheet's Size popup. A row id: document (the default, the document's own size, evened on the formats that need it), 1080p, 720p, 1440p or 2160p (the number is the SHORT edge and the long edge follows the document's aspect, rounded to even, so a vertical project's 1080p is 1080 × 1920), or preset for an image format's cap (gif and apng, where it is the default) — or an explicit [width, height] in px, the sheet's Custom…. Every row exists on every document — a 1920 × 1080 project's 1080p is its own size and is listed anyway — so an id means the same thing wherever it is sent; only preset is confined to gif and apng. Omit for the document size or the image format's cap; a wrong id's error lists the ids. A movie is written at exactly the size asked for, letterboxed if the shape differs by more than a pixel (within a pixel the picture fills the frame instead) — except on prores_4444, hevc_alpha and vp9_alpha_webm, where a size of a DIFFERENT SHAPE from the canvas is refused rather than written, because the padding destroys the alpha channel those rows are chosen for; every row id is already the document's own shape, so this only bites on a [width, height] of another shape. h264, hevc, hevc_alpha, hevc_10bit, av1, av1_webm, vp9_webm, vp9_alpha_webm and prores_422 need even dimensions: an odd width or height — the document size on a canvas with an odd edge, or an odd [width, height] — is written one pixel smaller on that edge, and the width and height reported back are the file's. The shape is judged on that even frame: a [width, height] in the canvas's own proportions is never refused for being odd, at any scale; one of another shape is judged as the even size it becomes, which is the size hevc_alpha's refusal na
  - audio_bitrate : {"description": "Target AAC or Opus rate in BITS per second (192000 is 192 kbps) — the sheet's Quality popup. Omit for 192 kbps. Ignored where the sound is neither: ProRes, WAV and AIFF all write LPCM, whose rate is its sample format.", "type": "integer", "minimum": 1}
  - path : {"type": "string", "description": "Where to write, e.g. ~/Movies/cut.mp4. The folder must exist."}
  - range : {"description": "What to write: the whole timeline (default), or just the in/out range set_range set — the sheet's Range popup.", "enum": ["project", "in_out"], "type": "string"}
  - encoder_speed : {"type": "string", "description": "av1's and av1_webm's encoder speed — the sheet's Speed popup: faster (SVT-AV1 preset 8), balanced (preset 6, the default) or smaller (preset 4, the smallest file for the picture and the slowest). Refused on every other preset, vp9_webm and vp9_alpha_webm included (libvpx has one speed).", "enum": ["faster", "balanced", "smaller"]}
  - video_bitrate : {"minimum": 1, "type": "integer", "description": "Target video rate in BITS per second (8000000 is 8 Mbps) — the sheet's Quality popup. Omit to let the preset's own encoder settings decide. Ignored by presets that carry no rate of their own: ProRes is fixed by its profile, and GIF and APNG have no bitrate at all. On av1 and av1_webm it is a soft cap: SVT-AV1 encodes at a constant quality matched to HEVC at this rate (HEVC's 0.07 bits per pixel per frame when omitted), and the file usually stays under it; very detailed footage can go up to about 1.25 times over. On vp9_webm it is a soft cap too: libvpx encodes at a constant quality matched to HEVC at this rate (HEVC 10-bit's in an HDR export), and the file usually stays under it; up to about 1.15 times it was measured; on vp9_alpha_webm the cap is the picture's alone, its alpha at a constant quality."}
  - burn_in_timecode : {"type": "boolean", "description": "Draw the project timecode over the picture, topmost — the export sheet's Burn in timecode checkbox. Default: false. It is a review copy's reference, so it reads the DOCUMENT'S timecode in the document's own spelling (the toolbar's, non-drop, HH:MM:SS:FF or HH:MM:SS.mmm), and it shows PROJECT time even in a file written from the in/out range: a range export starting at 00:00:10:00 begins at 00:00 carrying a burn-in that reads 00:00:10:00, which is the number a note about that frame needs."}
  - stems : {"type": "string", "enum": ["tracks", "names"], "description": "Also write stems beside the mix, on audio_m4a, audio_wav and audio_aiff — the sheet's Stems popup: tracks (one file per heard timeline track) or names (heard tracks whose names match, ignoring case, share one file). Each is named '<file> - <track name>.<ext>' (an unnamed track 'Track N'), replacing any file there, and holds its tracks' sound before the master: no loudness gain and no limiter, so the stems sum to the mix where the master changed nothing. Muted and solo-silenced tracks have none. Refused on a movie preset, when no track is heard, and when a stem's path is one of the project's media. Omit for none."}

## set_video : Changes the clips' BASE video properties — position, scale, rotation, opacity, crop, corner radius, shadow, colour, chroma key, filters, reflection, spotlight and motion (what the clip does on its way in, on its way out and all the way through). The patch MERGES AT EVERY DEPTH: keys you leave out keep their values, inside a nested object too — a patch of just {shadow: {blur: 20}} keeps the shadow's colour, angle and distance — and shadow, chroma, reflection, spotlight or motion set to null clears it. A LIST is the exception: filters and obscure each replace whole, so send the whole stack and [] to clear it. A key or a value the vocabulary does not have is an error naming its dotted path (array indices included) and the value you sent; nothing is dropped, corrected or clamped. Placement is spec §5.3: the source rectangle is centred at the canvas centre plus (x, y), scaled, then rotated about that centre, and crop removes edges in place without shifting the content. To animate a property instead, add_action and then set_action_end. On an adjustment clip only color, wheels, curves, look, the point filters and opacity (the grade's strength) do anything, the qualifier narrows which pixels they reach, and blur, sharpen, pixelate or a blend mode is an error. cutout (Remove Background) is taken by media and still clips only, and behind_subject (Text Behind Subject) by any drawing clip but an adjustment or nest clip.
  - clips : {"description": "Clip ids (get_project lists them).", "minItems": 1, "items": {"type": "integer", "minimum": 0}, "type": "array"}
  - patch : {"type": "object", "description": "Video properties to merge into the clip's base props. Keys: x, y (canvas px from the canvas centre, y DOWN), scale (1 = native pixels, 0.01–10), rotation (degrees, positive turns clockwise on screen), opacity (0–1), crop ({l, t, r, b} in SOURCE px — cropping never shifts the content), corner_radius (source px), shadow (null or {color, opacity, blur, angle, distance}), color ({brightness, contrast, saturation, hue, exposure, temperature, tint, highlights, shadows, whites, blacks, vibrance, fade}, each 0 = unchanged and any subset merging — exposure is -3 to 3 (EV), fade 0–1 and the other seven tone keys -1 to 1, refused outside; a positive temperature is warmer, a positive tint more magenta; hue is in degrees, and a positive hue turns every colour's HSV hue that far, red → yellow → green, keeping its Rec. 709 luma), chroma (null or {key: [r, g, b], tolerance, softness, spill}), filters (an ARRAY of {kind, amount} applied in order, at most 4 — kind is blur|sharpen|pixelate|sepia|noir|vignette|invert and amount is 0–1; send the whole array, since it replaces rather than merges, and [] clears the stack), reflection (null or {opacity, height, gap, falloff} — height and falloff are fractions of the layer's cropped height, gap is source px), spotlight (null or {shape: rect|ellipse, x, y, width, height, feather, dim, zoom} — the centre and the box are SOURCE px from the cropped rect's centre, dim is how much darker it is OUTSIDE and zoom 1–2 magnifies what is inside), obscure (an ARRAY of {shape: rect|ellipse, x, y, width, height, feather, kind, amount} that HIDES what is under each one, at most 4 — kind is blur|pixelate and amount is 0–1, feather is the source px the effect ramps in over, and the centre and the box are SOURCE px from the crop
## set_audio : Changes the clips' BASE audio properties — volume, mute, fades, ducking, mono, pitch preservation, pan, compression and noise reduction. Merges like set_video; duck or compressor set to null removes it. To animate one, use an action's set_action_end: volume, mute, the fades and every key the sound's effects read animate there (pan, mono, channels, eq, hum, noise_reduction, gate, leveller, de_esser, compressor, limiter, isolation); duck, keep_pitch, the fade shapes and voice are this tool's alone.
  - patch : {"type": "object", "description": "Audio properties to merge. Keys: volume (0–32, 1 = unity), muted, fade_in, fade_out (ticks), fade_in_shape and fade_out_shape (linear — the default — s_curve, plus_3db for a fast start, minus_3db for a slow one), duck (null or {level, attack, release, mode, pre_roll, hold}: mode extent — the default, for the clip's whole length — or speech, only while the clip's speech plays, widened by pre_roll before and hold after, in ticks, 0.5 s each by default; speech saves the media's speech spans from its detected speech in the same step, and is an error for a clip that is not a media clip with sound or whose speech has not been detected yet), mono, keep_pitch, pan (-1 hard left … +1 hard right), compressor (null or {threshold_db, ratio, attack, release, makeup_db} — the two times in ticks), noise_reduction (0–1), limiter (null or {ceiling_db, release}: a true-peak limiter last in the clip's chain, ceiling_db in dBTP after the clip's base volume, -24 to 0, default -1, release in ticks, 600 to 600000, default 60000; {limiter: {}} turns it on at the defaults), isolation (0–1: voice isolation, Apple's on-device voice model, which also reduces room echo; 0 off, 0.8 the app's default, 1 leaves silence between words; rendered offline, the clip plays without it until the render lands and an export waits for it; a clip not at ×1 plays without it), channels (both — the default — left, right or swapped: left and right play that channel of a stereo source from both sides, swapped exchanges them; first in the clip's chain after isolation, ignored on a mono source), eq (null or {high_pass, low_shelf, band_1, band_2, band_3, high_shelf, low_pass, bypass}, after the channel pick: each slot null (off) or an object; high_pass and low_pass {frequency, slope}, fr
  - clips : {"minItems": 1, "description": "Clip ids (get_project lists them).", "type": "array", "items": {"type": "integer", "minimum": 0}}
## set_text : Changes a text clip's words and typography. Merges like set_video; outline, backdrop and width set to null clear them (a null width restores auto-sizing). Position and scale are set_video's, not this tool's.
  - patch : {"description": "Text properties to merge. Keys: content, font (a PostScript name), size (px), color, align (left|center|right), valign (top|middle|bottom), line_spacing, tracking, outline (null or {width, color}), backdrop (null or {color, padding, radius}), width (null for auto-sizing, or a wrap width in px), animation (null or {kind: typewriter|word_fade|line_rise, in, out} — how the words arrive and leave. in and out are DURATIONS in ticks measured from the clip's own first and last frame, not times; either may be 0 for no ramp at that end, and in + out longer than the clip simply shows a partial reveal, and both are snapped to the document's frame grid. Like shadow and backdrop it MERGES: a key you leave out keeps what is stored, and {animation: null} clears the whole thing), case (as_written|upper — applied before layout; it never changes the word count), words (one {start, end} per whitespace token of content, in clip ticks, or [] for an untimed page — a content change that alters the count must send words: [] in the same patch), word_style (null or {mode: highlight|build, active_color, active_scale 1–1.5, active_pill {color, padding, radius, glide}, spoken_color, upcoming_color, active_fill, pause_dim 0–1} — how the word being said is drawn; it replaces animation. Three looks, each off by default: active_pill.glide (true|false) slides the pill from the word before to the active one over 0.2 s, or sooner when the next word starts sooner, instead of jumping; active_fill (true|false) fills the active word left to right from its start to its end, taking 0.6 s at most — the part said in active_color and the rest in upcoming_color, or not yet drawn in build mode; in highlight mode it shows only where those two colours differ; pause_dim fades the words and the pill to 
  - clips : {"minItems": 1, "description": "Clip ids (get_project lists them).", "type": "array", "items": {"type": "integer", "minimum": 0}}
## set_shape : Changes a shape clip's geometry and paint. Merges like set_video; stroke or fill set to null removes that half of the paint. Position and scale are set_video's, not this tool's. Changing kind changes ONLY the kind — an arrow made this way keeps whatever arrow_end it had, unlike one created by add_shape_clip.
  - clips : {"type": "array", "items": {"type": "integer", "minimum": 0}, "minItems": 1, "description": "Clip ids (get_project lists them)."}
  - patch : {"type": "object", "description": "Shape properties to merge. Keys: kind (rectangle|ellipse|line|arrow), width, height (the box, in source px), stroke (null or {width, color}), fill (null or a colour), radius (rectangle corners), arrow_start, arrow_end, and from / to — the endpoints of a line or arrow as UNIT coordinates in its box, e.g. from [0, 1] to [1, 0], gradient (null, or {kind: linear|radial, stops: [{at, color}], angle} — a gradient FILL, which takes precedence over fill while it is set and leaves it in place, so {\"gradient\": null} restores the colour underneath. 2 to 8 stops, each at 0–1 along the ramp; the stops need NOT be sorted, and the renderer sorts them. angle is degrees from the RIGHT turning counter-clockwise, the convention shadow's angle uses, and means nothing for radial, which is an ellipse inscribed in the box. line and arrow have no area and ignore it. Like stroke it MERGES: a key you leave out keeps what is stored, {gradient: null} clears it and brings the flat fill back out, and the stops are a LIST, so sending one replaces them all). A colour is FOUR numbers, [r, g, b, a], each 0–1: three is refused by length and a component outside the range is refused by name, neither filled in nor clamped."}
## transcribe : Transcribes clips and lays the result out as TEXT CLIPS, one per cue, on a new track of its own — File ▸ Generate Captions…. They are ordinary text clips in the Caption preset, exactly what import_captions makes, so they render in the preview, in render_frame and in an export (burn-in) and can be retimed with move_clip or restyled with set_text. Cues are at most two lines of 42 characters and at most 6 s, with a frame between them. A word caption STYLE (every style but subtitles) makes PAGES instead: each page is filled to the width it will MEASURE on this canvas in that style — at most 0.86 of the canvas width — not to a word count, on one line (karaoke: up to two); a page also closes at a change of speaker, a gap of 0.25 s or more, a word ending . ? ! or …, 3 s on screen, and one_word's single word; a lone word too wide shrinks to 80% before it wraps. Pages stay within their own clip and abut the next page where the gap is under 0.25 s, else hold 0.5 s after the last word. Each placed page is a text clip in the style's face, case and word_style carrying its words' timings, so the word being said is drawn as the style says. The result carries the cues themselves in TICKS whether or not they were placed — with a word style each cue adds words, [{start, end, text}] in timeline ticks, and size where a lone word shrank — names the engine that ran, reports under skipped every clip you named that cannot be transcribed, and, whenever replace was given, lists under replaced the tracks it removed. slide, fill and dim each add a look to a word style's pages; whenever one of the three was given, a placed run lists under looks the ones that were on. ENGINE "apple" (the default) is ON-DEVICE speech recognition and nothing leaves this Mac. It needs no grant and has no length ceiling. Its models are installed per Mac: a language whose model is not installed is refused by name and nothing is changed — this tool never downloads one; a person installs it from File ▸ Generate Captions…'s Download row. ENGINE "assemblyai" sends the audio of every media file behind the clips you named — the WHOLE file, not just the parts in use — to AssemblyAI, a cloud service that transcribes it and is billed for it, and the transcript is deleted from AssemblyAI once the words have been read back; do not choose it for somebody else's recording without their say-so. New transcription needs an API key; a nonempty batch whose every media has a current packaged transcript can be reused without a key or network request, unless refresh is true. Pending remote deletion is reported as deferred until a later run with the originating account and region's key can safely retry it. It needs no grant and caches one transcript per media file inside the project package, so a second run over the same files makes no request and reports them under cached. It adds uploaded, cached, audio_seconds (what was billed), speech_model_used, language_detected, endpoint (the host alone of the server a keyed run's requests go to; absent when no API key is set) and, when there is something to say, note. Keyed (spec §11 CJ-3): where it would send to AssemblyAI it needs the person's AssemblyAI API key, and without one it refuses there with "No AssemblyAI API key is set. Set one in Frame Job ▸ Settings… ▸ Transcription.".
  - style : {"enum": ["subtitles", "highlight", "pill", "karaoke", "build", "one_word"], "type": "string", "description": "subtitles (default: two-line cues in the Caption preset, unchanged) or a word caption style that shows each word as it is said: highlight (Montserrat Black, upper case, the word in yellow with a pop), pill (Montserrat ExtraBold, upper case, the word on a violet pill), karaoke (Montserrat ExtraBold as written, up to two lines, words turning yellow), build (Anton, upper case, words appearing one by one) or one_word (Bebas Neue, one large word at a time). Anything else is refused with the six named. A style is a display choice: a cached transcript is reused whatever style is asked for, and restyling is running transcribe again with replace."}
  - vocabulary : {"type": "array", "items": {"type": "string"}, "description": "engine \"assemblyai\" only. Words and phrases to expect — product names, people, identifiers. Trimmed, de-duplicated keeping the first spelling, and capped at 1000 terms with the cap named in note. Refused by name with engine \"apple\"."}
  - slide : {"description": "The pill slides from the word before to the word being said, over 0.2 s or sooner, instead of jumping: writes word_style.active_pill.glide true on every page placed. Default false, and never what the sheet last chose. It applies with style pill: true is refused by name with any other style or with place false, where it would show nothing; false is always accepted (unlike replace, which is refused whenever it is given where it cannot apply).", "type": "boolean"}
  - language : {"type": "string", "description": "A language tag, e.g. en-US. WITH apple: this Mac's own language by default. The two ways a tag can be wrong are two different problems with two different fixes, so they are refused SEPARATELY and the message says which you have. A tag speech recognition has never heard of is a misspelling: it is refused with the tags it does know. A tag it knows whose on-device MODEL is not installed on this Mac is refused as not installed, with the languages ready here: a person has to install it from the Generate Captions sheet, and this tool never downloads. WITH assemblyai: \"auto\" (the default) detects it and the result reports what was detected and how sure it was; a code is passed through unchecked, and one AssemblyAI does not support comes back as its own sentence after the upload."}
  - clips : {"description": "Transcribe only these clips — the sheet's scope. Omit for every clip in the project that has recorded sound. Only a clip playing a movie or sound file can be transcribed, and not one played in REVERSE, whose words would come out backwards; a clip you name that cannot be is reported under skipped.", "type": "array", "minItems": 1, "items": {"type": "integer"}}
  - refresh : {"description": "engine \"assemblyai\" only. Ignore the cached transcript and upload again (default false). Refused by name with engine \"apple\", which has no cache.", "type": "boolean"}
  - place : {"type": "boolean", "description": "Place the cues as text clips (default true), as ONE undo step. false returns the cues and changes nothing at all — no edit, no undo step — which is the way to check a transcript before committing to it."}
  - speakers : {"description": "engine \"assemblyai\" only. Ask for speaker labels (default false); a change of speaker then starts a new cue, and with style subtitles each speaker's cues take their own colour (white, yellow, cyan, green in order of first appearance, then again; a speaker is a label within one media file). Refused by name with engine \"apple\".", "type": "boolean"}
  - dim : {"description": "The page fades to half its opacity in a pause of 0.25 s or more: writes word_style.pause_dim 0.5 on every page placed. On-device (engine apple) words rarely carry a pause that long, so it shows mostly with assemblyai. Default false, and never what the sheet last chose. It applies with style highlight, pill, karaoke, build or one_word: true is refused by name with any other style or with place false, where it would show nothing; false is always accepted (unlike replace, which is refused whenever it is given where it cannot apply).", "type": "boolean"}
  - replace : {"description": "With a word style and place true only — refused by name with subtitles or with place false. true removes, in the SAME undo step, every caption track this command could have made over these clips: named Captions or Captions N, holding only text clips, each of which overlaps a clip being transcribed. The result lists them under replaced.", "type": "boolean"}
  - engine : {"type": "string", "description": "Where the recognition happens: apple (default) on this Mac, assemblyai in the cloud. Anything else is refused with both spellings named. The default is NEVER what the sheet last chose — an agent that wants the cloud says so every time.", "enum": ["apple", "assemblyai"]}
  - fill : {"description": "The word being said fills left to right as it is spoken, taking 0.6 s at most: writes word_style.active_fill true on every page placed. Default false, and never what the sheet last chose. It applies with style highlight, karaoke or build: true is refused by name with any other style or with place false, where it would show nothing; false is always accepted (unlike replace, which is refused whenever it is given where it cannot apply).", "type": "boolean"}
## export : Starts writing the timeline to a file and answers AT ONCE with {export_id, path, status} — ⌘E, the export sheet's six controls (format, size, frame rate, quality, loudness, range) in the sheet's own vocabulary, all but the frame rate — this exports at the document's rate, or on gif and apng at the format's own rate (12.5 and 25 fps). The export is a job in the Export Queue, run one at a time behind any other export already there; status is queued or running. An av1, av1_webm or vp9_webm job also waits while the app's other AV1 and VP9 exports would hold more than a quarter of this Mac's memory with it, and a later job may run first meanwhile (export_status's waiting says so). Follow it with export_status, which never waits — call it again after a pause until status is done, failed or cancelled — and stop it with cancel_export. The export_id lasts until Frame Job quits. Every refusal comes back from this call, before anything is queued: a bad argument, a missing folder, and a graphic clip whose module does not compile; a failure while writing comes back from export_status, failed with its reason (a graphic that fails during the export stops it with no file left, naming the graphic and the frame). The preset is the ENCODING only and the size a separate choice defaulting to the document's own, or on gif and apng to the format's cap; the core resolves both into concrete settings (container, codec, size, rate, range) and hands them to the same exporter the sheet uses, so an agent export and a menu export cannot disagree. An existing file at the path is replaced, and the container's extension is added when the path has none. In an HLG project (set_canvas output) hevc_10bit, av1, av1_webm, vp9_webm, prores_422 and prores_4444 write HLG unless dynamic_range says pq or sdr, and every other video preset writes the project's SDR version. What the finished file holds is export_status's done answer.
  - peak_dbtp : {"type": "number", "minimum": -20, "maximum": 0, "description": "The ceiling of the master limiter every export with sound runs, in dBTP: the file's true peak stays at or under it. Omit for -1 (the sheet's fixed value); 0 is the loosest, and there is no off. Legal with or without loudness_lufs. How far the limiter turned the hottest peak down is the result's limited_db."}
  - audio_bits : {"description": "audio_wav's and audio_aiff's bit depth — the sheet's Bit depth popup: 16 (the default) or 24, rounded once from the float mix, never dithered. Refused on every other preset (ProRes keeps 16).", "type": "integer", "enum": [16, 24]}
  - preset : {"type": "string", "description": "Export preset id — one per encoding, a container and a codec with no size of its own (that is size's). h264 is what the export sheet opens on. Four rows keep a transparent canvas transparent: prores_4444, hevc_alpha and vp9_alpha_webm (the movie rows — see size, which constrains them) and apng. gif is not one of them: it can show a transparent pixel but has no alpha channel, so nothing partial survives it. prores_422, prores_4444 and hevc_10bit keep the compositor's 16-bit frames at 10 bits (ProRes 4444 at 12); h264, hevc and hevc_alpha are 8-bit. av1 is AV1 in an MP4 with AAC and av1_webm the same AV1 in a WebM with Opus, both 10-bit, encoded by the bundled SVT-AV1 at encoder_speed; Macs before M3 cannot play them back. vp9_webm is VP9 in a WebM with Opus from the bundled libvpx, at one speed: 8-bit (Profile 0) for an SDR picture, 10-bit (Profile 2) for HLG and PQ. vp9_alpha_webm is that VP9 with its alpha, always 8-bit (the SDR version in an HLG project), the transparent movie Chrome and Firefox play; Safari's is hevc_alpha. Pass a wrong one and the error lists them all."}
  - dynamic_range : {"description": "An HDR (HLG) project's picture on hevc_10bit, av1, av1_webm, vp9_webm, prores_422 and prores_4444 — the sheet's Dynamic Range popup: hlg (the default; BT.2100 HLG, no Dolby Vision), pq (HDR10: BT.2100 PQ at 1,000 cd/m², mastering display P3-D65 0.005–1,000 cd/m², MaxCLL 1,000, MaxFALL 0) or sdr (the SDR version). Every other video preset (vp9_alpha_webm too) writes the SDR version; hlg and pq are refused there and in an SDR project, where sdr changes nothing.", "type": "string", "enum": ["hlg", "pq", "sdr"]}
  - loudness_lufs : {"maximum": 0, "type": "number", "minimum": -70, "description": "Normalise the whole mix to this integrated loudness, in LUFS — the sheet's Loudness popup, whose rows are -14 (streaming), -16 (podcast) and -23 (EBU R 128 broadcast). The export measures itself first and then applies ONE gain to everything, solved through the master limiter every export runs at peak_dbtp, so the file lands on the target and its loudest peaks are limited rather than the whole mix capped. Omit not to normalise: the export still measures, still limits at the ceiling and still reports. Ignored where there is no sound to measure (gif, apng)."}
  - size : {"maxItems": 2, "minItems": 2, "description": "Output size — the sheet's Size popup. A row id: document (the default, the document's own size, evened on the formats that need it), 1080p, 720p, 1440p or 2160p (the number is the SHORT edge and the long edge follows the document's aspect, rounded to even, so a vertical project's 1080p is 1080 × 1920), or preset for an image format's cap (gif and apng, where it is the default) — or an explicit [width, height] in px, the sheet's Custom…. Every row exists on every document — a 1920 × 1080 project's 1080p is its own size and is listed anyway — so an id means the same thing wherever it is sent; only preset is confined to gif and apng. Omit for the document size or the image format's cap; a wrong id's error lists the ids. A movie is written at exactly the size asked for, letterboxed if the shape differs by more than a pixel (within a pixel the picture fills the frame instead) — except on prores_4444, hevc_alpha and vp9_alpha_webm, where a size of a DIFFERENT SHAPE from the canvas is refused rather than written, because the padding destroys the alpha channel those rows are chosen for; every row id is already the document's own shape, so this only bites on a [width, height] of another shape. h264, hevc, hevc_alpha, hevc_10bit, av1, av1_webm, vp9_webm, vp9_alpha_webm and prores_422 need even dimensions: an odd width or height — the document size on a canvas with an odd edge, or an odd [width, height] — is written one pixel smaller on that edge, and the width and height reported back are the file's. The shape is judged on that even frame: a [width, height] in the canvas's own proportions is never refused for being odd, at any scale; one of another shape is judged as the even size it becomes, which is the size hevc_alpha's refusal na
  - audio_bitrate : {"description": "Target AAC or Opus rate in BITS per second (192000 is 192 kbps) — the sheet's Quality popup. Omit for 192 kbps. Ignored where the sound is neither: ProRes, WAV and AIFF all write LPCM, whose rate is its sample format.", "type": "integer", "minimum": 1}
  - path : {"type": "string", "description": "Where to write, e.g. ~/Movies/cut.mp4. The folder must exist."}
  - range : {"description": "What to write: the whole timeline (default), or just the in/out range set_range set — the sheet's Range popup.", "enum": ["project", "in_out"], "type": "string"}
  - encoder_speed : {"type": "string", "description": "av1's and av1_webm's encoder speed — the sheet's Speed popup: faster (SVT-AV1 preset 8), balanced (preset 6, the default) or smaller (preset 4, the smallest file for the picture and the slowest). Refused on every other preset, vp9_webm and vp9_alpha_webm included (libvpx has one speed).", "enum": ["faster", "balanced", "smaller"]}
  - video_bitrate : {"minimum": 1, "type": "integer", "description": "Target video rate in BITS per second (8000000 is 8 Mbps) — the sheet's Quality popup. Omit to let the preset's own encoder settings decide. Ignored by presets that carry no rate of their own: ProRes is fixed by its profile, and GIF and APNG have no bitrate at all. On av1 and av1_webm it is a soft cap: SVT-AV1 encodes at a constant quality matched to HEVC at this rate (HEVC's 0.07 bits per pixel per frame when omitted), and the file usually stays under it; very detailed footage can go up to about 1.25 times over. On vp9_webm it is a soft cap too: libvpx encodes at a constant quality matched to HEVC at this rate (HEVC 10-bit's in an HDR export), and the file usually stays under it; up to about 1.15 times it was measured; on vp9_alpha_webm the cap is the picture's alone, its alpha at a constant quality."}
  - burn_in_timecode : {"type": "boolean", "description": "Draw the project timecode over the picture, topmost — the export sheet's Burn in timecode checkbox. Default: false. It is a review copy's reference, so it reads the DOCUMENT'S timecode in the document's own spelling (the toolbar's, non-drop, HH:MM:SS:FF or HH:MM:SS.mmm), and it shows PROJECT time even in a file written from the in/out range: a range export starting at 00:00:10:00 begins at 00:00 carrying a burn-in that reads 00:00:10:00, which is the number a note about that frame needs."}
  - stems : {"type": "string", "enum": ["tracks", "names"], "description": "Also write stems beside the mix, on audio_m4a, audio_wav and audio_aiff — the sheet's Stems popup: tracks (one file per heard timeline track) or names (heard tracks whose names match, ignoring case, share one file). Each is named '<file> - <track name>.<ext>' (an unnamed track 'Track N'), replacing any file there, and holds its tracks' sound before the master: no loudness gain and no limiter, so the stems sum to the mix where the master changed nothing. Muted and solo-silenced tracks have none. Refused on a movie preset, when no track is heard, and when a stem's path is one of the project's media. Omit for none."}

