## auto_zoom : Plans screencast zooms from screen recordings' cursor files and applies them as ONE undo step "Auto Zoom" — Clip ▸ Auto Zoom…. Each zoom is an ordinary action (add_zoom_action's shape). A visit starts at a down click (or, with typing, a typing stretch, aimed at the last click before it): a zoom-in arriving lead seconds before it, a pan for each later click outside the zoomed region's inner 60 %, with follow a 0.5 s pan when the pointer stays outside the inner 70 % for 0.3 s, and a 0.8 s zoom back stay seconds after the last activity (or at a scroll burst). A click within stay of the last joins the visit; a zoom back that would end under 1 s before the next zoom-in becomes a pan. The region has the canvas's aspect at zoom × the clip's scale, kept inside the picture. Halves of a split are planned as one: every zoom back ends at the first half's placement, nothing straddles a cut, and a zoom held across a cut sets the later half's base (bases_set) unless it has an action of its own there, when the visit closes at the cut (closed_at_cut). A planned zoom meeting an action already on the clip is dropped (dropped); with replace the clips' existing zooms are removed first (replaced) and never any other action. Each clip given a zoom whose own motion_blur is 0 is set to motion_blur (0.5 by default, a 180° shutter; set_video's key, drawn along the zooms) in the same step — blurred counts them; 0 sets none, and a clip that already has a shutter keeps it. Refused: a clip that is not a media clip, whose media has no cursor file or whose file is missing, that plays reversed, has no picture, or is placed smaller than the canvas (nest it and call auto_zoom inside the nest with set_scope). Answers visits, added, replaced, dropped, closed_at_cut, bases_set, blurred and actions [{clip, at, duration, kind}] (at in clip ticks; kind in, pan, follow or back); with nothing to zoom, ok with a note and no undo step.
