## speed_up_typing : Plays the typing in screen recordings faster, as ONE undo step "Speed Up Typing" — Clip ▸ Speed Up Typing…. A typing stretch is a run of at least 3 typed keys each under 1 s apart, read from the clips' cursor files and placed through each clip's speed and ramps. From each stretch is taken away every span of speech of every clip with sound under it, on any track, and each recorded shortcut with its overlay (its clip on a track named "Shortcuts", else 1.5 s), so speech and shortcut overlays always play at normal speed. The pieces of all the clips are joined where they meet, a piece shorter than shortest seconds is left alone (short counts them), and each kept piece is sped by retime_range at rate on every unlocked track, keeping 0.3 s at normal speed at each end, so everything after it moves up in step. Its sound is sped too, its pitch kept with keep_pitch. A media with sound under a stretch whose speech has not been saved gets its spans from on-device speech detection in the same step (speech_saved); while detection runs the call is refused — try again in a moment. Refused, whole and before anything changes: a clip that is not a media clip, whose media has no cursor file or whose file is missing, or that plays reversed; a rate or shortest out of range; any range retime_range would refuse (a locked track after it, a transition, a linked clip …), naming it. Answers stretches, short, ranges [{from, to, sped_to}] in container ticks before the edit, saved (ticks), speech_saved and, when a clip with sound is now above the audio ceiling, audio_left_out and audio_note; with nothing to speed, ok with a note and no undo step.
